  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how PFAS and their destruction byproducts, labeled PIDs, can move through the environment and create human exposure pathways. Blue arrows trace PFAS transport and red arrows trace PID transport from multiple sources, including industries, water treatment residuals, municipal waste, and decentralized or centralized destruction technologies. Emissions from destruction units rise to the air, and both PFAS and PIDs appear to undergo long-range transport followed by wet and dry deposition back onto land and water.\n\nOnce released, contaminants spread through runoff and leaching into rivers and groundwater. The diagram highlights several routes of bioaccumulation: PFAS seem to enter fish and agricultural crops, which then contribute to human intake through eating contaminated food. A pond or river area also indicates recreational exposure, while people near industrial release sites may inhale contaminated air. PFAS-impacted sites and firefighting foam use are shown as additional sources feeding contamination into surface water and subsurface pathways.\n\nOverall, the image presents a connected source-to-exposure network, emphasizing that both PFAS and PIDs can persist, disperse widely, and reach people through air, water, food, and direct contact with contaminated environments.",
